'''Bashmuric''' (bash-mö′rik), ''n.'' A dialect of Coptic, named from the district Bashmur of Lower Egypt, in the eastern part of the Delta: as, the ''Bashmuric'' version of the New Testament. Also ''Basmuric.''

'''basi-.''' The combining form, in various scientific terms, of Latin ''basis'' (Gr. βάσις), base. See ''basis.''

'''basia''' (bā′zi-ä̤), ''n. pl.'' [Lit. kisses; pl. of L. ''basium'', a kiss.] A name for erotic verses or amorous writings of any kind; anacreontics; sapphics: as, the ''basia'' of Bonnefons and Secundus.

'''basial''' (bā′zi-ạl), ''a.'' [< L. ''basium'', a kiss, + ''-al.''] Relating to or consisting of a kiss. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|The innocent gaiety of his sister-in-law expressed itself in the “funny answers” and the ''basial'' salutation.
{{right|''Quarterly Rev.''}}}}

